Mathematical Foundations of Truth‑Seeking Intelligence

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek,Grok

Abstract

We construct the formal core of an epistemic agent that transforms raw observations into logically traceable, statistically calibrated, risk‑bounded, and robustly convergent outputs. The architecture is a single composite object

(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, B_t, \mathcal{U}_E, \mathcal{B}_t, \mathcal{J}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{V}_\alpha)

where \Omega is the state space, \mathcal{F} a \sigma-algebra of propositions, B_t the Bayesian belief measure, \mathcal{U}_E the update operator, \mathcal{B}_t the explicit belief base, \mathcal{J} the justification category, \mathcal{G} the evidence graph, \mathcal{R} the epistemic risk function, and \mathcal{V}_\alpha the verified output set.
We disentangle truth, belief, logical consequence, and statistical confidence; formalise a memory‑to‑knowledge pipeline; and derive a unified risk budget that fuses model uncertainty, data reliability, proof‑trace completeness, and calibration error.
Robustness to adversarial evidence is achieved via a generalised Bissiri–Holmes–Walker update with trust‑weighted loss, and we prove almost‑sure convergence of the robust posterior under mild conditions, closing the main gap in earlier versions. Information geometry provides the natural gradient for optimal belief transport, and computational hardness is explicitly bounded.
The Grand Unifying Theorem states that, under declared assumptions, every output in \mathcal{V}_\alpha is simultaneously logically grounded, distribution‑free calibrated, risk‑bounded, adversarially robust up to a contamination fraction, and convergent. The chapter concludes with a full register of assumptions and a precise catalogue of what the architecture does not claim.

