1. Introduction

An artificial intelligence that seeks truth — not merely to mimic human text or maximise a reward — must rest on a mathematical foundation that treats belief, evidence, reasoning, and verification as rigorously as physics treats energy and force. This chapter provides that foundation. It is the first component of a larger thesis that will later extend the single‑agent architecture to multi‑agent verification (PhD 2) and to energetic constraints via Landauer’s principle (PhD 3). Here we restrict ourselves to the individual cognitive engine: how can a machine form, update, and certify its beliefs in a way that is mathematically transparent and provably sound under specified assumptions?

The architecture diagram commonly drawn in high‑level roadmaps contains boxes labelled “Knowledge”, “Bayesian Belief State”, “Reasoning Engine”, “Uncertainty Quantification”, and “Verified Output”. Our task is to turn every arrow in that diagram into a theorem. We achieve this by building a single composite mathematical object, ( \Omega, \mathcal{F}, B_t, \mathcal{U}_E, \mathcal{B}_t, \mathcal{J}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{V}_\alpha ), and proving that each component satisfies the required properties simultaneously.

The literature already supplies many of the necessary pieces: Bayesian probability theory (Cox, de Finetti), measure‑theoretic foundations (Kolmogorov), conformal prediction (Vovk, Gammerman, Shafer), belief revision (AGM), categorical proof theory, variational inference, and information geometry (Amari). Our contribution is their coherent fusion, the explicit separation of truth from belief, the risk budget integrating model, data, reasoning, and calibration uncertainty, and the extension of convergence guarantees to the robust, trust‑weighted belief update that real systems require.

The chapter is structured as follows. Section 2 sets up the state space and the algebra of propositions. Section 3 defines the Bayesian belief state and the unique consistent updating rule. Section 4 separates the four distinct concepts — truth, belief, logical consequence, and statistical confidence — and proves they cannot collapse into one another. Section 5 builds the memory‑to‑knowledge pipeline and introduces the explicit/implicit belief distinction. Section 6 develops the proof‑trace category and the justification graph. Section 7 defines the epistemic risk function and the verified output set. Section 8 formalises evidence objects and the trust‑weighted robust update, and proves the convergence of the generalised posterior. Section 9 adds the causal reasoning layer and the limits of counterfactual identifiability. Section 10 equips the belief manifold with the Fisher metric and proves the optimality of natural gradient. Section 11 collects convergence, stability, and computational hardness results. Section 12 extends the architecture to multi‑scale reasoning, the scientific discovery loop, and thermodynamic constraints. Section 13 specifies the human oversight gate. Section 14 lists all assumptions explicitly. Section 15 summarises the limitations. Section 16 presents the Grand Unifying Theorem. Section 17 proposes benchmark dimensions. Section 18 concludes.

