4. The Four Distinct Concepts: Truth, Belief, Logical Consequence, Confidence

A common critique of Bayesian epistemology is that it conflates belief with truth. We forestall this by making the distinction precise and provable within the formalism.

Definition 4.1 (Objective Truth). There exists a fixed but unknown actual world \omega^* \in \Omega. The truth of proposition A is the indicator \mathbf{1}_{\omega^* \in A} \in \{0,1\}. The agent never observes \omega^* directly.

Definition 4.2 (Evidence‑Supported Belief). B_t(A) is the agent’s degree of belief as defined in Section 3.

Definition 4.3 (Logical Consequence). For propositions A, B, we write A \models B if every model satisfying A also satisfies B (i.e., A \subseteq B in the set representation). This relation lives in the justification category \mathcal{J} (Section 6) and is independent of any probability.

Definition 4.4 (Statistical Confidence). A conformal predictor provides, for a chosen error level \alpha \in (0,1), a prediction set C(x) with the guarantee  \mathbb{P}[y \in C(x)] \ge 1-\alpha  over exchangeable trials (Theorem 5.4). This is a frequentist property of the procedure, not a probability of any single claim.

Theorem 4.1 (Non‑collapse). The four objects are provably distinct:

1. B_t(A) = 1 does not imply \mathbf{1}_{\omega^* \in A} = 1 (a perfectly confident agent can still be wrong).
2. A \models B does not require B_t(A) > 0 (logical consequence holds vacuously from impossible premises).
3. A valid (1-\alpha) conformal guarantee is a marginal statement; it allows individual prediction sets to be arbitrarily inaccurate on a measure‑zero subset and does not assign a probability to any particular claim’s truth.

Proof. (1) Take any well‑calibrated but misspecified model: e.g., the agent’s prior/likelihood are such that B_t(A)=1 but the true data‑generating process gives \omega^* \notin A. (2) Let A = \emptyset; then \emptyset \models B trivially, but B_t(\emptyset) = 0. (3) Conformal guarantee: \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_{y \in C(x)}] = 1-\alpha is compatible with \mathbf{1}_{y \in C(x)} = 0 on a set of zero probability. ∎

Thus “truth” enters the formalism only as the unattainable \omega^*; the architecture bounds and calibrates belief, it does not claim to verify truth in the correspondence sense. This is an explicit limitation, not a hidden flaw.

