5. Memory, Knowledge, Belief, and Verified Output

Raw sensor data are epistemically inert until validated. We introduce a strict filter.

Definition 5.1 (Memory Layer). \mathcal{M} is a multiset of recorded observations \{o_i\}, carrying no truth‑tracking guarantee.

Definition 5.2 (Validation Predicate). A predicate \mathcal{V}(o) outputs 1 iff an observation o meets criteria such as source reliability above a threshold, internal consistency with the current belief base, and non‑contradiction with established facts. The criteria are themselves learned and updated via a second‑order Bayesian process over sources.

Definition 5.3 (Knowledge Filter \kappa). \kappa: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}) maps memory to a belief base \mathcal{B}_t \subseteq \mathcal{F} by \mathcal{B}_t = \{ o \in \mathcal{M} : \mathcal{V}(o) = 1 \}.

Theorem 5.1 (Knowledge ⊊ Memory). \kappa is non‑injective and non‑surjective; |\mathcal{B}_t| \le |\mathcal{M}| with strict inequality whenever any observation fails validation. Thus knowledge is a proper subset of memory.

Definition 5.4 (Explicit vs. Implicit Belief).

· A proposition A is explicitly believed iff A \in \mathcal{B}_t.
· A proposition A is implicitly believed iff it belongs to the deductive closure \text{Cn}(\mathcal{B}_t) under the agent’s inference rules.

This distinction is crucial for proof traces: only implicit beliefs with a derivation chain from \mathcal{B}_t can enter the verified output set.

The pipeline becomes:

\mathcal{M} \xrightarrow{\kappa} \mathcal{B}_t \xrightarrow{\text{Def 3.1, closure}} B_t \xrightarrow{\text{Section 7 filter}} \mathcal{V}_\alpha.

Each arrow is a formally defined operator, not a hand‑wave.

---

6. Proof Traces as Categorical Structure

To guarantee “no hidden reasoning assumptions”, every verified output must carry an explicit derivation from the belief base.

Definition 6.1 (Justification Category \mathcal{J}).

· Objects: propositions in \text{Cn}(\mathcal{B}_t).
· Morphisms: a morphism f: A \to B exists iff there is a single valid inference step deriving B from A and possibly other premises in \mathcal{B}_t.
· Composition: chaining of inference steps; associativity holds because the order of applying independent inference rules does not affect the final derived proposition.
· Identity: the trivial zero‑step derivation.

Definition 6.2 (Proof Trace). A proof trace for A is a morphism in \mathcal{J} from a subset of \mathcal{B}_t (the “axioms”) to A. If no such morphism exists — i.e., the only path to A would require an unlabelled arrow — A is inadmissible for verified output.

This recasts logical grounding in category‑theoretic terms: the reasoning engine is a functor from evidence to conclusions, and every step is explicitly typed.

We will later (Section 8) extend this to a larger evidence graph \mathcal{G} that includes supports, contradicts, and causes edges, absorbing \mathcal{J} as its “implies” subgraph.

---

7. Confidence with Guarantees: Conformal Calibration

Bayesian degrees of belief are numbers; they need not be trustworthy. Conformal prediction provides a distribution‑free, finite‑sample calibration layer that certifies predictions without assuming the model is correct.

Definition 7.1 (Calibration Set). Let \mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}} = \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^{n} be held‑out input‑output pairs, exchangeable with future test points.

Definition 7.2 (Nonconformity Score). A function \nu: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y} \to \mathbb{R} measuring how atypical a candidate answer y is for input x under the current belief state. Lower scores indicate better conformity.

Definition 7.3 (Conformal Threshold). For a target error rate \alpha \in (0,1), define the empirical quantile

\hat{\tau}_\alpha = \text{Quantile}\!\left( \frac{\lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil}{n} \right) \text{ of } \{ \nu(x_i, y_i) \}_{i=1}^{n}.

Theorem 7.1 (Distribution‑Free Coverage — Vovk et al. 2005). If \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^{n+1} are exchangeable, then the prediction set

C(x_{n+1}) = \{ y : \nu(x_{n+1}, y) \le \hat{\tau}_\alpha \}
\]  

satisfies

1-\alpha \;\le\; \mathbb{P}\bigl[ y_{n+1} \in C(x_{n+1}) \bigr] \;\le\; 1-\alpha + \frac{1}{n+1}.

Proof sketch. Under exchangeability, the rank of the nonconformity score of (x_{n+1}, y_{n+1}) among all n+1 scores is uniformly distributed. The event y_{n+1} \in C(x_{n+1}) corresponds to the rank being at most \lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil. The probability of this is exactly \lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil / (n+1), which lies in the stated interval. ∎

This guarantee holds irrespective of the quality of the underlying belief state B_t. It provides the “uncertainty quantification” box with a mathematically rigorous foundation.

