8. Verified Output and the Epistemic Risk Function

Confidence calibration alone is insufficient; a claim may be well‑calibrated but rest on a single unreliable source or lack a proof trace. We therefore define a composite risk and a threshold‑based filter.

Definition 8.1 (Epistemic Risk Decomposition). For a candidate output A \in \text{Cn}(\mathcal{B}_t), the total risk is

\mathcal{R}(A) = \lambda_1 R_{\text{model}}(A) + \lambda_2 R_{\text{data}}(A) + \lambda_3 R_{\text{reasoning}}(A) + \lambda_4 R_{\text{calibration}}(A),
\]  

with \lambda_i \ge 0, \sum \lambda_i = 1. The components are:

· R_{\text{model}}(A): epistemic uncertainty — the variance of B_t(A) under a Bayesian posterior over model parameters (or across an ensemble).
· R_{\text{data}}(A): 1 - \text{reliability-weighted support} from the evidence graph (Section 8).
· R_{\text{reasoning}}(A): 1 - \mathbf{1}[A \text{ has a complete proof trace in } \mathcal{J}], a hard 0/1 term: ungrounded claims are categorically inadmissible.
· R_{\text{calibration}}(A): the rescaled nonconformity score \nu(x,A) from Section 7, mapped to [0,1].

Definition 8.2 (Verified Output Set). For a chosen risk budget \rho \in (0,1),

\mathcal{V}_\alpha = \{ A \in \text{Cn}(\mathcal{B}_t) : \mathcal{R}(A) \le \rho \}.

This replaces a naive “most confident claim” criterion: the system must minimise total epistemic risk, not just output well‑calibrated guesses. A claim can be well‑calibrated (low R_{\text{calibration}}) but data‑poor (high R_{\text{data}}), and the risk budget catches that.

