9. Robust Evidence Integration and Trust‑Weighted Updating

The exact Bayesian update of Theorem 3.1 assumes every piece of evidence is perfectly reliable and uncorrupted. Real environments contain adversarial or low‑quality information. We replace the likelihood update with a generalised, trust‑weighted framework that provably limits the influence of any single source.

Definition 9.1 (Evidence Object). Each piece of evidence is a tuple

E = (s, r, t, c),
\]  

where s \in \mathcal{S} is a source identifier, r \in [0,1] a reliability weight (itself estimated with uncertainty), t \in \mathbb{R} a timestamp, and c \in \mathcal{F} the context proposition under which the evidence was collected.

Definition 9.2 (Trust‑Weighted Generalised Update). Following Bissiri, Holmes & Walker (2016), we define the updated belief state via a loss function \ell(\theta; E) and a calibration parameter \beta > 0:

\pi_n(\theta) \;\propto\; \pi(\theta) \,\exp\!\Bigl( -\beta \sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \,\ell(\theta; E_i) \Bigr),
\]  

where \pi(\theta) is a prior density over the parameter \theta of the belief state (e.g., parameters of a neural network). Ordinary Bayesian conditioning is recovered when \ell is the negative log‑likelihood and \beta = 1.

Theorem 9.1 (Robustness Bound). If \ell is chosen from the family of robust divergences (e.g., \beta-divergence, \gamma-divergence, Hellinger distance), then the influence of a single corrupted evidence item E^* on \pi_n is bounded independently of the magnitude of E^*’s deviation. In contrast, the standard Bayes update can be arbitrarily swayed by a single sufficiently extreme (or replicated) observation.

Proof sketch. For a robust divergence, the derivative of \ell with respect to an outlier saturates, so the gradient contribution in the exponential is bounded. Formally, if \ell(\theta; E) \le M for all E and \theta, then the ratio \pi_n(\theta)/\pi_n(\theta') can change by at most a factor of \exp(2\beta r^* M) when adding E^*, regardless of its extremeness. ∎

9.1 Convergence of the Robust Posterior

A crucial gap in earlier versions was the lack of a convergence guarantee for the generalised update. We now provide one.

Assumptions 9.A (for convergence).

1. The observations E_i are exchangeable.
2. The parameter space \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^d is compact and the true data‑generating process has a unique minimiser \theta^* of the expected loss L(\theta) = \mathbb{E}[\ell(\theta; E)].
3. The loss \ell is strictly proper and continuous in \theta for all E.
4. The prior \pi has a positive, continuous density on an open neighbourhood of \theta^*.
5. The reliability weights r_i are bounded below by a positive constant for a non‑null subset of the data, ensuring that cumulative weight grows without bound.

Theorem 9.2 (Almost‑Sure Convergence of Robust Posterior). Under Assumptions 9.A, the generalised posterior \pi_n concentrates at \theta^*:

\pi_n\bigl( \{ \theta: \|\theta - \theta^*\| > \varepsilon \} \bigr) \xrightarrow{a.s.} 0 \qquad \forall \varepsilon > 0.

Proof sketch. Define the normalised weight R_n(\theta) = \exp(-\beta \sum_{i=1}^n r_i \ell(\theta; E_i)). Consider the random measure \Pi_n(d\theta) \propto R_n(\theta)\pi(\theta)d\theta. The log‑ratio of the posterior mass outside a neighbourhood to that inside is

\log \frac{\Pi_n(\Theta \setminus N_\varepsilon)}{\Pi_n(N_\varepsilon)} = \log \frac{\int_{\Theta \setminus N_\varepsilon} R_n(\theta) \pi(\theta) d\theta}{\int_{N_\varepsilon} R_n(\theta) \pi(\theta) d\theta}.
\]  

Divide numerator and denominator by R_n(\theta^*) (the weight at the true minimiser). Under a strong law of large numbers for the weighted sum \frac{1}{n}\sum r_i \ell(\theta; E_i), the exponent converges to -\beta \bar{r} L(\theta) for each \theta, where \bar{r} is the average reliability. Because \theta^* is the unique minimiser, for \theta \notin N_\varepsilon we have L(\theta) > L(\theta^*) + \delta for some \delta>0. The denominator contains a neighbourhood of \theta^* where L(\theta) \approx L(\theta^*). A standard application of the bounded convergence theorem and the positivity of the prior around \theta^* shows the ratio tends to 0 almost surely. Convergence in probability and almost‑sure weak consistency follow; with further regularity one can obtain rates. ∎

Thus the robust update does not sacrifice long‑run convergence; it merely shifts the target from the KL minimiser under a true likelihood to the minimiser of the expected robust loss — typically the same \theta^* under well‑specified models, and a stable approximation otherwise. In the Grand Unifying Theorem we will reference both the standard (Theorem 3.1 + martingale argument) and robust convergence regimes, made explicit.

