10. The Evidence Graph

Definition 10.1 (Evidence Graph \mathcal{G}). A directed graph (\mathcal{N}, \mathcal{E}) where nodes are hypotheses, observations, experiments, and edges are labelled by one of \{ \text{supports}, \text{contradicts}, \text{implies}, \text{causes} \}.

· implies edges correspond exactly to morphisms in \mathcal{J} (Definition 6.1).
· causes edges correspond to directed edges in the structural causal model (Section 11).
· contradicts edges allow the system to represent live disagreement: a proposition A is inadmissible for \mathcal{V}_\alpha if it has an incoming contradicts‑edge from another element in \mathcal{B}_t with higher reliability‑weighted support.

This graph absorbs the justification category as a subgraph and adds the richer evidential and causal relations that a real scientific assistant must model.

---

11. Causal Reasoning Layer

Belief states and evidence updates capture association, not intervention. A separate causal module is necessary to answer “what if” questions.

Definition 11.1 (Structural Causal Model — SCM). A tuple \langle V, U, \mathcal{F}, P_U \rangle:

· V: endogenous variables.
· U: exogenous (latent) variables with distribution P_U.
· \mathcal{F} = \{ f_V \}: a function for each V \in V that computes its value from its causal parents and an exogenous noise term.

The observational distribution P(V) is obtained by propagating P_U through \mathcal{F}. The interventional distribution P(V \mid do(X=x)) is obtained by replacing the structural equation for X with the constant x and propagating the noise. These two distributions are distinct objects, and in general the interventional distribution is not identifiable from the observational distribution alone.

Theorem 11.1 (Counterfactual Non‑identifiability). Even with perfect knowledge of the causal graph and the observational distribution, counterfactual probabilities are not uniquely determined without further structural assumptions (e.g., mechanism‑wise invertibility and context‑independent inverse transport, as in Nasr‑Esfahany & Kocaoglu 2023). Global monotonicity is sufficient but not necessary.

Therefore, any causal claim admitted into \mathcal{V}_\alpha must carry an explicit tag specifying which structural assumption licensed the identification. Un‑tagged causal statements are treated as incomplete proof traces and excluded. This is a direct application of the “no hidden reasoning assumptions” principle to causality.

