12. Information Geometry of Belief Transport

Bayesian updating moves the belief state. We now equip the space of belief states with a natural geometry that respects the structure of probability distributions.

Definition 12.1 (Belief Manifold). The set \mathcal{M} = \{ B_\theta : \theta \in \Theta \} of parameterised probability measures on (\Omega, \mathcal{F}) is a statistical manifold.

Definition 12.2 (Fisher Information Metric).

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{B_\theta}\!\left[ \frac{\partial \log B_\theta}{\partial \theta_i} \frac{\partial \log B_\theta}{\partial \theta_j} \right].

This metric appears as the second‑order Taylor expansion of the Kullback‑Leibler divergence:

\text{KL}(B_\theta \| B_{\theta + d\theta}) = \frac{1}{2} g_{ij}(\theta) d\theta_i d\theta_j + O(\|d\theta\|^3).

Theorem 12.1 (Chentsov’s Uniqueness, 1982). The Fisher metric is, up to a scalar factor, the only Riemannian metric on the manifold of probability distributions that is invariant under sufficient statistics (i.e., under the transformation of the data space). This makes it the canonical geometry for belief states.

Definition 12.3 (Natural Gradient). The steepest descent direction of a loss L(\theta) on the belief manifold is given by

\tilde{\nabla} L(\theta) = g(\theta)^{-1} \nabla L(\theta).

Theorem 12.2 (Convergence Rate). Natural gradient descent on the ELBO (or, with appropriate metric, on the robust loss) achieves asymptotically Fisher‑efficient convergence: the belief update trajectory approaches the optimum at the best possible rate for any first‑order method, following a geodesic‑like path on \mathcal{M}.

Thus the earlier variational inference (Section 3.1’s scalable version) is upgraded from a Euclidean heuristic to a geometrically natural procedure. In the robust regime, the metric should be replaced by the Hessian of the expected robust loss; analogous properties hold under regularity conditions.

