13. Stability and Convergence of the Belief Sequence

A truth‑seeking agent must eventually settle on a belief if evidence accumulates indefinitely and consistently.

Theorem 13.1 (Martingale Convergence under Exact Bayesian Updating). Let \{B_t\} be the sequence of posterior measures obtained by repeated Bayesian conditioning on an exchangeable sequence of observations generated by a fixed true parameter \theta^*. Assume the model is correctly specified and the prior assigns positive mass to every open neighbourhood of \theta^*. Then for any measurable set A, \{B_t(A)\} is a bounded martingale and converges almost surely. Moreover, B_t converges weakly to a point mass at \theta^* (posterior consistency). If the model is misspecified, B_t converges to the point mass at the parameter \theta^\dagger that minimises the KL divergence to the true data‑generating distribution.

Proof. Apply Doob’s martingale convergence theorem to the likelihood ratio process; for consistency, use Schwartz’s theorem and posterior concentration rates (Ghosal & van der Vaart). ∎

Corollary 13.2 (Divergence conditions). Convergence fails if (a) the prior excludes the truth (Cromwell’s rule violation), (b) the evidence stream is not exchangeable (adversarial ordering breaks the martingale property), or (c) a growing fraction of evidence is corrupted (see Theorem 9.1). These conditions are the explicit failure modes of exact Bayesian updating.

Theorem 13.3 (Convergence under Robust Update). Under Assumptions 9.A, the generalised posterior \pi_n (Definition 9.2) converges almost surely to a point mass at the minimiser of the expected robust loss, as shown in Theorem 9.2. This holds even when the standard likelihood is misspecified, provided the robust loss has a unique population minimiser.

The architecture therefore offers two regimes: exact Bayes (Theorem 3.1, convergent under strong trust) and robust Bayes (Theorem 9.2, convergent under weaker trust but with bounded adversarial influence). The agent must declare which regime is active, and the risk budget reflects the choice.

