14. Computational Complexity and Tractable Approximation

Theorem 14.1 (Hardness of Exact Inference). Computing B_t(A) exactly in a general Bayesian network is #P‑complete (Roth 1996). Approximating posterior probabilities within a fixed relative error is NP‑hard in the worst case (Dagum & Luby 1993). Therefore, no polynomial‑time algorithm exists for either exact or approximate general inference unless P = NP.

This compels a tractability boundary. Exact inference is feasible only for restricted graph structures (e.g., bounded treewidth). At civilisation scale, one must use the variational approximation (ELBO optimisation, Section 3.1), whose convergence guarantees are conditional (Theorem 12.2, Theorem 13.3 under a tractable robust loss). Every output in \mathcal{V}_\alpha must be tagged with the inference regime — exact or approximate — and the assumptions that underpin its approximate convergence. This tag itself becomes part of the proof trace.

