15. Multi‑Scale Reasoning, Scientific Discovery, and Thermodynamic Constraints

To connect with later PhD chapters, we outline how the architecture extends to multiple granularities and to a complete scientific loop.

Definition 15.1 (Scale Functor). Let \mathcal{J}_{\text{token}}, \mathcal{J}_{\text{sentence}}, \mathcal{J}_{\text{document}}, \mathcal{J}_{\text{graph}}, \mathcal{J}_{\text{theory}} be justification categories at increasing granularities. A scale functor F: \mathcal{J}_k \to \mathcal{J}_{k+1} maps propositions and proof morphisms to coarser equivalents while preserving composition and identity. This provides the formal link between low‑level reasoning steps and high‑level scientific conclusions.

Definition 15.2 (Scientific Discovery Operator). The cycle Observation → Hypothesis → Prediction → Experiment → Update is a composite operator

\mathcal{D} = \text{Update} \circ \text{Experiment} \circ \text{Predict} \circ \text{Hypothesize} \circ \text{Observe}
\]  

acting on the belief state. This is a literal function composition over \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{B}_t, not a flowchart.

Definition 15.3 (Epistemic Efficiency). The thermodynamic cost of updating belief is measured by

\eta = \frac{\Delta H}{\Delta E},
\]  

where \Delta H = H(B_t) - H(B_{t+1}) is the reduction in Shannon entropy of the belief state and \Delta E is the energy consumed (Joules). Landauer’s principle sets a lower bound: erasing one bit of uncertainty costs at least k_B T \ln 2. This ties directly into PhD 3’s resource‑bounded reasoning.

