18. Limitations (What the Architecture Does Not Claim)

Honesty requires stating the boundaries explicitly:

1. Truth is unobservable. The system bounds belief, it does not verify truth in the correspondence sense (Theorem 4.1).
2. Convergence is to the model’s best approximation, not necessarily to \omega^*. Under misspecification, the belief concentrates at the KL‑closest (or robust‑loss‑minimising) parameter, not the true world (Theorem 13.1).
3. Causal claims are not identifiable without untestable structural assumptions (Theorem 11.1). Every causal output carries an identification‑assumption tag.
4. Exact inference is computationally intractable in general (Theorem 14.1). Approximate inference trades optimality for tractability and has only conditional convergence guarantees.
5. Robustness and exact likelihood cannot coexist. The robust update (Theorem 9.1) abandons the strict Bayesian interpretation of Theorem 3.1; one must choose the update regime and accept its associated cost.
6. The conformal guarantee is marginal, not conditional. It does not provide instance‑level confidence for every possible input, only an aggregate coverage statement (Theorem 7.1).

