20. The Grand Unifying Theorem

Theorem 20.1 (Truth‑Seeking Intelligence Theorem).
Let the agent be described by the architecture (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, B_t, \mathcal{U}_E, \mathcal{B}_t, \mathcal{J}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{V}_\alpha) operating under Assumptions (A1)–(A8) as relevant to each component. Suppose the agent adopts either the exact Bayesian update (Theorem 3.1) or the robust trust‑weighted update (Theorem 9.2) with a declared choice and satisfies its corresponding convergence assumptions (A3–A4 or 9.A). Then for any output A \in \mathcal{V}_\alpha:

1. Logical traceability: A has a complete proof trace from \mathcal{B}_t in \mathcal{J} (Section 6).
2. Statistical calibration: The procedure yielding A is distribution‑free calibrated at level 1-\alpha (Theorem 7.1).
3. Risk‑boundedness: \mathcal{R}(A) \le \rho with the risk decomposed into model, data, reasoning, and calibration terms (Section 8).
4. Robustness to adversarial evidence: Under the robust update, the influence of any finite set of corrupted observations is bounded; under the exact update, the system is vulnerable (degradation bounded by contamination fraction — Theorem 9.1).
5. Convergence: The belief sequence \{B_t\} or \{\pi_n\} converges almost surely to a limit B^* (Theorems 13.1 and 13.3). If the model is correctly specified (exact regime) or the robust loss minimiser equals the true parameter, B^* = \delta_{\omega^*}; otherwise it concentrates at the best approximation.

Proof. Each item has been proved separately in the cited sections. The theorem’s force is that these properties hold simultaneously when the corresponding assumptions are satisfied. The architecture makes the regime explicit; thus any output is accompanied by a trace of which assumptions were used. ∎

What the theorem does not claim: truth of A (Theorem 4.1), identifiability of causal content without structural assumptions (Theorem 11.1), or tractability of exact computation (Theorem 14.1). These limitations are the precise handoff points to PhD 2’s multi‑agent verification and to PhD 3’s resource‑bounded reasoning.

