Appendix A. Foundations of Probability and the Uniqueness of Bayesian Conditioning

A.1 Cox’s Theorem (Statement)

Cox’s theorem (Cox 1946, Jaynes 2003) shows that any function that assigns real numbers to propositions and satisfies:

1. \text{Pl}(A \mid X) is a function of \text{Pl}(\neg A \mid X);
2. \text{Pl}(A \land B \mid X) is a function of \text{Pl}(A \mid X) and \text{Pl}(B \mid A \land X);
   and a minimal consistency condition, is order‑isomorphic to a probability measure. Consequently, probability theory is the unique calculus of plausible inference under those desiderata. We take this as a warrant for using probability measures to represent degrees of belief.

A.2 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Uniqueness of Conditioning)

Theorem 3.1 (Uniqueness of Bayesian updating).
Let B be a probability measure on (\Omega,\mathcal{F}). Suppose an update operator \mathcal{U}_E maps B to a new measure B' satisfying:

· (i) B'(E)=1;
· (ii) For all A,A'\subseteq E, \frac{B'(A)}{B'(A')} = \frac{B(A)}{B(A')} (rigidity);
· (iii) B' is a probability measure.

Then for every A\in\mathcal{F},

B'(A) = \frac{B(A\cap E)}{B(E)}.

Proof.
From (ii), for any A\subseteq E there is a constant k independent of A such that B'(A)=k\,B(A). Setting A=E in (i) yields 1 = B'(E) = k\,B(E), hence k = 1/B(E) (note that B(E)>0 is implied by (i) together with the fact that B' is a probability measure; formally, if B(E)=0 then B'(E)=k\cdot0=0, contradicting (i); thus B(E)>0).

Now take an arbitrary A\in\mathcal{F}. Split A = (A\cap E) \cup (A\cap E^c). Because B'(E)=1, we have B'(E^c)=0; by monotonicity of probability, B'(A\cap E^c)=0. Therefore

B'(A) = B'(A\cap E) = \frac{B(A\cap E)}{B(E)},

which is the standard conditional probability. The representation holds for all A and satisfies countable additivity because B' is required to be a probability measure. ∎

This theorem shows that any updating rule that accepts the evidence fully, preserves relative plausibilities within the evidence set, and yields a valid probability distribution must coincide with Bayes’ rule. Any alternative “reasoning assumption” hidden in the update would violate (i)–(iii).

