Appendix B. The Conformal Calibration Guarantee

B.1 Exchangeability and the Conformal Prediction Theorem

Definition B.1 (Exchangeability). A sequence of random variables Z_1,\dots,Z_n is exchangeable if for every permutation \sigma the joint distribution of (Z_{\sigma(1)},\dots,Z_{\sigma(n)}) is identical to that of (Z_1,\dots,Z_n).

Theorem 7.1 (Distribution‑free coverage).
Let (X_i,Y_i)_{i=1}^{n+1} be exchangeable random pairs. For a chosen nonconformity measure \nu: \mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to\mathbb{R} and error rate \alpha\in(0,1), define the empirical quantile

\hat\tau_\alpha = \text{the } \lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1)\rceil\text{-th smallest value among } \{\nu(X_i,Y_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}.

Then the prediction set for the new object X_{n+1} is

C(X_{n+1}) := \{ y : \nu(X_{n+1}, y) \le \hat\tau_\alpha \},

and it satisfies

\mathbb{P}\bigl[ Y_{n+1} \in C(X_{n+1}) \bigr] \;\ge\; 1-\alpha,
\qquad
\mathbb{P}\bigl[ Y_{n+1} \in C(X_{n+1}) \bigr] \;\le\; 1-\alpha + \frac{1}{n+1}.

Proof.
Define the nonconformity scores S_i = \nu(X_i,Y_i) for i=1,\dots,n+1. Because the data are exchangeable, the vector (S_1,\dots,S_{n+1}) is also exchangeable. Under the null hypothesis that the new point follows the same distribution, the rank of S_{n+1} among all n+1 scores is uniformly distributed over \{1,\dots,n+1\}.
The event Y_{n+1}\in C(X_{n+1}) is equivalent to S_{n+1} \le \hat\tau_\alpha. By definition, \hat\tau_\alpha is the k-th smallest of the first n scores, where k = \lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil. The inequality S_{n+1} \le \hat\tau_\alpha holds exactly when S_{n+1} is not larger than the k-th smallest of the other scores, i.e. when its rank among all n+1 scores is at most k. The number of ranks \le k is k, so

\mathbb{P}[S_{n+1} \le \hat\tau_\alpha] = \frac{k}{n+1} = \frac{\lceil (1-\alpha)(n+1) \rceil}{n+1}.

The right‑hand side lies between 1-\alpha and 1-\alpha + 1/(n+1). This completes the proof. ∎

The coverage statement is marginal over the randomness of the calibration and test points; it requires no knowledge of the true distribution of (X,Y) and holds even if the nonconformity scores are poorly calibrated. The only assumption is exchangeability.

