Appendix C. Robust Evidence Integration

C.1 General Bayesian Updating with Loss Functions

Bissiri, Holmes, and Walker (2016) show that for any proper loss function \ell(\theta; E) connected to an observation E, updating a prior \pi(\theta) via

\pi_n(\theta) \propto \pi(\theta) \exp\!\bigl(-\beta \sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \ell(\theta; E_i)\bigr)

provides a coherent updating rule that does not require a full likelihood. The parameter \beta>0 governs the rate of learning. Standard Bayesian conditioning is recovered when \ell is the negative log‑likelihood and \beta=1.

C.2 Proof of Theorem 9.1 (Bounded Influence of a Corrupted Observation)

Theorem 9.1 (Robustness bound).
If the loss \ell is chosen from the family of robust divergences (e.g., \beta-divergence, \gamma-divergence) such that \ell(\theta; E) \le M < \infty for all \theta, E, then the influence of a single contaminated evidence item E^* on the posterior \pi_n is bounded. Precisely, for any measurable set A,

\left| \pi_n(A) - \pi_n^{(E^*)}(A) \right| \le 2\bigl( e^{\beta r^* M} - 1 \bigr),

where \pi_n^{(E^*)} is the posterior obtained by adding an extra observation E^* with reliability weight r^*. In contrast, under a standard likelihood update (where \ell can be unbounded), a single extreme observation can move the posterior arbitrarily close to an arbitrary target.

Proof.
Let L_n(\theta) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \ell(\theta; E_i). The standard posterior without E^* is \pi_n(\theta) \propto \pi(\theta) e^{-\beta L_n(\theta)}. After including the extra observation, the weight becomes e^{-\beta (L_n(\theta) + r^* \ell(\theta; E^*))} = e^{-\beta L_n(\theta)} e^{-\beta r^* \ell(\theta; E^*)}. Because 0 \le \ell(\theta; E) \le M, we have

e^{-\beta r^* M} \le e^{-\beta r^* \ell(\theta; E^*)} \le 1.

Thus the ratio of the posterior densities satisfies

e^{-\beta r^* M} \;\le\; \frac{\pi_n^{(E^*)}(\theta)}{\pi_n(\theta)} \cdot \frac{Z_n}{Z_n^{(E^*)}} \;\le\; 1,

where Z_n, Z_n^{(E^*)} are the normalising constants. Hence for any set A,

\pi_n^{(E^*)}(A) = \frac{\int_A e^{-\beta r^* \ell(\theta;E^*)} d\Pi_n(\theta)}{\int_\Omega e^{-\beta r^* \ell(\theta;E^*)} d\Pi_n(\theta)},

with \Pi_n the probability measure \pi_n. Both numerator and denominator differ from those of \pi_n(A) by at most a factor e^{\pm \beta r^* M}. Consequently, the absolute difference is bounded as stated. ∎

For standard Bayes (likelihood), the factor can be arbitrarily large, so the posterior can be completely dominated by an outlier.

C.3 Proof of Theorem 9.2 (Almost‑Sure Convergence of the Robust Posterior)

We prove a rigorous version of the convergence result sketched in the main text.

Assumptions C.1.

· (C1) \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^d is compact.
· (C2) The loss \ell(\theta; e) is continuous in \theta for every e and there exists a dominating function D(e) with \mathbb{E}[D(E)]<\infty such that |\ell(\theta; e)| \le D(e).
· (C3) The true data‑generating process for E_1,E_2,\dots is exchangeable and ergodic; the expected loss L(\theta) = \mathbb{E}[\ell(\theta; E)] has a unique global minimiser \theta^*.
· (C4) The prior \pi has a density that is continuous and strictly positive on an open neighbourhood of \theta^*.
· (C5) The reliability weights r_i are strictly positive, bounded, and satisfy \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \to \bar{r} > 0 almost surely.

Theorem 9.2 (Robust posterior consistency).
Under assumptions (C1)–(C5), the sequence of robust posteriors \pi_n(\theta) \propto \pi(\theta)\exp(-\beta \sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \ell(\theta; E_i)) concentrates at \theta^* almost surely: for every \varepsilon>0,

\lim_{n\to\infty} \pi_n\bigl( \{\theta : \|\theta-\theta^*\| > \varepsilon\} \bigr) = 0 \quad \text{a.s.}

Proof.
Define the weighted empirical loss \hat{L}_n(\theta) = \frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^n r_i}\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i \ell(\theta; E_i). Let S_n = \sum_{i=1}^n r_i. By (C2) and the law of large numbers for exchangeable arrays (or ergodic theorem), for each fixed \theta, \hat{L}_n(\theta) \to L(\theta) almost surely. Moreover, because \Theta is compact and \ell is continuous, the convergence is uniform (a straightforward application of the uniform law of large numbers for dependent data using bracketing or the fact that an exchangeable sequence is stationary; see, e.g., Theorem 16(a) in Pollard 1984). Hence,

\sup_{\theta\in\Theta} | \hat{L}_n(\theta) - L(\theta) | \xrightarrow{a.s.} 0.

Let \varepsilon>0. Define the neighbourhood N = \{\theta : \|\theta-\theta^*\| < \varepsilon/2\} and its complement N^c. Because L is continuous and \theta^* is the unique minimiser, there exists \delta>0 such that

\inf_{\theta\in N^c} L(\theta) \ge L(\theta^*) + \delta, \qquad \sup_{\theta\in N} L(\theta) \le L(\theta^*) + \delta/4.

From the uniform convergence, for almost all realisations there is an n_0 such that for all n\ge n_0,

\sup_{\theta\in\Theta} | \hat{L}_n(\theta) - L(\theta) | < \delta/4.

Then

\inf_{\theta\in N^c} \hat{L}_n(\theta) \ge L(\theta^*) + \frac{3\delta}{4}, \qquad
\sup_{\theta\in N} \hat{L}_n(\theta) \le L(\theta^*) + \frac{\delta}{2}.

Now write the posterior mass of N^c as

\pi_n(N^c) = \frac{ \int_{N^c} e^{-\beta S_n \hat{L}_n(\theta)} \pi(\theta) d\theta }
{ \int_\Theta e^{-\beta S_n \hat{L}_n(\theta)} \pi(\theta) d\theta }.

For n\ge n_0,

\int_{N^c} e^{-\beta S_n \hat{L}_n(\theta)} \pi(\theta) d\theta
\le e^{-\beta S_n ( L(\theta^*) + 3\delta/4 )} \pi(N^c).

For the denominator, restrict the integral to the smaller neighbourhood N_{\varepsilon/2} = \{\theta: \|\theta-\theta^*\|<\varepsilon/2\} (which is contained in N) where the prior density is bounded below by some c>0 (by (C4)). On this set, \hat{L}_n(\theta) \le L(\theta^*) + \delta/2, so

\int_\Theta e^{-\beta S_n \hat{L}_n(\theta)} \pi(\theta) d\theta
\ge \int_{N_{\varepsilon/2}} e^{-\beta S_n (L(\theta^*) + \delta/2)} \pi(\theta) d\theta
\ge c \cdot \text{Vol}(N_{\varepsilon/2}) \cdot e^{-\beta S_n (L(\theta^*) + \delta/2)}.

Therefore,

\pi_n(N^c) \le \frac{ \pi(N^c) }{ c\,\text{Vol}(N_{\varepsilon/2}) } \;
e^{-\beta S_n (3\delta/4 - \delta/2)} = C\, e^{-\beta S_n \delta/4},

where C is a finite constant. Since S_n \to \infty almost surely (weights are strictly positive and average tends to \bar{r}>0), the bound tends to zero. This shows \pi_n(N^c) \to 0 a.s. Because N^c contains \{\theta: \|\theta-\theta^*\| > \varepsilon\} for sufficiently large n, the result follows. ∎

Thus the robust posterior enjoys the same consistency property as the ordinary Bayesian posterior under misspecification, but the target \theta^* is now the minimiser of the robust loss, which is typically the same as the true parameter when the model is well specified.

