Appendix D. Convergence of the Exact Bayesian Posterior

D.1 Martingale Convergence and Posterior Consistency (Theorem 13.1)

Theorem 13.1 (Bayesian posterior convergence).
Let X_1, X_2, \ldots be exchangeable and generated by a fixed true distribution P_{\theta^*} from a parameterised family \{P_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\}. Assume the prior \Pi has a density positive on a neighbourhood of \theta^* and that the model is correctly specified. Then for any measurable set A, the sequence of posterior probabilities \Pi(A \mid X_1,\dots,X_n) converges almost surely to \mathbf{1}_{A}(\theta^*) (weak posterior consistency).

Proof.
By Doob’s martingale convergence theorem, for any fixed measurable set A, the sequence M_n = \Pi(A \mid X_{1:n}) is a bounded martingale under the filtration generated by the data; hence it converges almost surely to a limit M_\infty. One then shows (using the fact that the true distribution is absolutely continuous with respect to the prior predictive) that M_\infty = \mathbf{1}_{\theta^*\in A} almost surely. The standard argument (see Ghosal & van der Vaart 2017) relies on the identifiability of \theta and the assumption that the prior puts positive mass on Kullback‑Leibler neighbourhoods of \theta^*. ∎

If the model is misspecified, the posterior concentrates at the parameter \theta^\dagger that minimises the Kullback‑Leibler divergence \text{KL}(P_0 \| P_\theta), where P_0 is the true data‑generating process. The proof uses the same martingale argument and an extended identifiability condition.

D.2 Failure Modes

The martingale property fails when the data are not exchangeable (e.g., adversarially ordered), breaking the convergence. Furthermore, if the prior assigns zero mass to a neighbourhood of the true \theta^* (Cromwell’s rule), the posterior can never concentrate at the truth.

