Appendix E. Variational Inference and the ELBO

E.1 Derivation of the ELBO

Let the true posterior over a latent variable z given evidence E be p(z|E). We approximate it with a variational family q_\theta(z). The Kullback‑Leibler divergence is

\text{KL}(q_\theta \| p(\cdot|E)) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log q_\theta(z)] - \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log p(z|E)].

Using p(z|E) = p(E,z)/p(E), we get

\text{KL}(q_\theta \| p(\cdot|E)) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log q_\theta(z)] - \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log p(E,z)] + \log p(E).

Rearranging,

\log p(E) = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log p(E,z)] - \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log q_\theta(z)]}_{\text{ELBO}(\theta)} + \text{KL}(q_\theta \| p(\cdot|E)).

Since \text{KL} \ge 0, we have \log p(E) \ge \text{ELBO}(\theta) and maximising the ELBO minimises the KL divergence to the true posterior. The optimal q_\theta is the true posterior exactly when the KL divergence vanishes.

E.2 Global Convergence Under Log‑Concavity (Theorem 1.4 in earlier notes)

Theorem. If \log p(E,z) is a concave function of z, and the variational family q_\theta is chosen such that the ELBO is a concave function of \theta (e.g., exponential families with natural parameterisation), then any local maximum is global, and gradient ascent on the ELBO converges to the global optimum.

Proof sketch. The concavity of the log‑joint implies that the log‑marginal \log p(E) is log‑concave and the posterior is log‑concave. The ELBO as a function of \theta equals \mathbb{E}_{q_\theta}[\log p(E,z)] + H(q_\theta). Under appropriate parameterisation, the entropy H(q_\theta) is convex, and the expected log‑joint is affine in the expectation parameters, yielding a concave objective. Standard results from convex optimisation then guarantee global convergence. ∎

