Appendix F. Information Geometry and the Natural Gradient

F.1 The Fisher Information Metric and Chentsov’s Uniqueness

Given a parametric family of probability densities p(x; \theta), the Fisher information matrix is

g_{ij}(\theta) = \int p(x;\theta) \frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta_i} \frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta_j} dx.

Chentsov’s theorem (1982) states that the only Riemannian metric on the manifold of probability distributions that is invariant under sufficient statistics (i.e., under Markov embeddings) is a scalar multiple of the Fisher metric. This makes it the canonical geometric structure for statistical inference.

F.2 Natural Gradient Descent and Fisher Efficiency

Theorem 12.2 (Natural gradient efficiency).
When minimising a loss L(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{true}}}[-\log p(x;\theta)] (the cross‑entropy), the natural gradient descent update

\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \eta \, g(\theta_t)^{-1} \nabla L(\theta_t)

achieves asymptotically the same convergence rate as the Newton method and is Fisher‑efficient, i.e., the estimator sequence attains the Cramér‑Rao lower bound in the limit.

Proof sketch (Amari 1998). In the continuous‑time limit, the natural gradient flow \dot\theta = -g(\theta)^{-1}\nabla L(\theta) is equivalent to the gradient flow on the Riemannian manifold. For the log‑likelihood loss, \nabla L(\theta) = g(\theta)(\theta - \theta^*) + o(\|\theta-\theta^*\|) near the optimum \theta^*, so the natural gradient direction g^{-1}\nabla L points directly toward \theta^*, eliminating the metric distortion. Consequently, the convergence is linear and asymptotically as fast as the Newton direction, which is optimal among first‑order methods. ∎

