Appendix G. Computational Hardness of Exact Inference

Theorem 14.1 (Complexity bounds).
(i) Computing the exact marginal probability of a query in a Bayesian network is #P‑complete (Roth 1996).
(ii) Even approximating the posterior marginal within a constant relative error is NP‑hard (Dagum & Luby 1993).

Proof. The proofs are by reduction from counting satisfying assignments of a 3‑SAT formula. Because exact inference is a counting problem, it inherits the complexity class #P. The hardness of approximation is shown via a stochastic simulation reduction. We refer the reader to the original papers for complete constructions. ∎

These results force a strict separation between tractable exact inference (bounded treewidth) and general inference, and justify the use of approximate variational or sampling‑based methods.

