Appendix H. Causal Identifiability of Counterfactuals

Theorem 11.1 (Non‑identifiability of counterfactuals).
Given a structural causal model \langle V,U,\mathcal{F},P_U\rangle and the observational distribution P(V), the counterfactual probability P(Y_x = y \mid X = x', Y = y') (the probability that Y would be y had X been set to x, given that we observed X=x', Y=y') is not uniquely determined from the observational distribution and the causal graph alone, without further structural assumptions.

Proof (special case). Consider a simple model with binary variables X, Y and a latent confounder U. The structural equations:

X = f_X(U, \varepsilon_X), \quad Y = f_Y(X, U, \varepsilon_Y).

The observational distribution P(X,Y) constrains the functions and noise distribution only through the joint. There exist multiple choices of f_X, f_Y and P_U that generate identical P(X,Y) yet yield different counterfactuals P(Y_1 = 1 \mid X=0, Y=0). Nasr‑Esfahany & Kocaoglu (2023) provide necessary and sufficient conditions (mechanism‑wise invertibility and context‑independent inverse transport) for identifiability; without these, the counterfactual is provably not identifiable. ∎

Therefore, any causal statement from the architecture must be accompanied by an explicit declaration of the structural assumptions used.

